timestamp=2026-11-09T08:12:50.453Z level=INFO run=5a234059 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# ST060 has many NaNs - 9 total. Also looks quite shifted? The pattern seems similar but timing off.\n# \"broadly follows local patterns, but not with the same rhythm as its peers\" - this fits a station that has a consistent phase shift relative to neighbors.\n# That could be a timing/sync issue or sensor clock drift. ST060 shows best correlation at lag -2 (8 hours)? That’s a big shift.\n# Or look at the shape - peaks appear earlier or later? Also compare with peers.\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.stats as stats\n\nstations = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/stations.csv')\nreadings = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/readings.csv')\npm10 = readings[readings['pollutant']=='PM10']\npm10_pivot = pm10.pivot_table(index='timestamp', columns='station_id', values='current_reading').sort_index()\nst_info = stations.set_index('station_id')\n\nst = 'ST060'\nd = st_info.loc[st, 'district']\npeers = st_info[st_info['district']==d].index.intersection(pm10_pivot.columns)\npeers_ref = [p for p in peers if p != st]\nref = pm10_pivot[peers_ref].median(axis=1, skipna=True)\nt = pm10_pivot[st]\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'t': t, 'ref': ref}).dropna()\nprint('lag0:', stats.pearsonr(df['t'], df['ref'])[0])\n# lag -2: t leads ref by 2 steps? If lag<0 in our earlier definition gave better corr at -2, that means when we align t.iloc[:lag] with ref.iloc[-lag:] for lag=-2, we got better corr. Or depends on definition.\n# Or more directly, corr(t_{i}, ref_{i+k}) - max at negative k means t is leading.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
